// ---- providers/v1/fleet/fleet.memql ----

/// The user's own machines (epic memql#4676). One base provider, and
/// DELIBERATELY NO per-model children.
///
/// Every other provider in this file is a record somebody wrote down: a
/// vendor, a model id, a price. A fleet model is none of those. It exists
/// while a laptop is awake and stops existing when the lid closes, and WHICH
/// laptop is a different answer for every user -- so a static child would be a
/// claim this file cannot keep. Models are named from a policy as
/// `fleet:<modelId>` (`@primary("fleet:llama3.1:8b")`) and resolved against
/// the live catalog at SELECTION time, never at load: an asleep fleet must not
/// refuse boot, and a machine waking up must not need a reload to be usable.
///
/// UNAVAILABLE IS A NORMAL STATE HERE, and it is the same answer for two
/// situations -- no machine offers the model, and none that offers it can do
/// what this prompt needs (structured output, embeddings). Both mean the
/// provider cannot serve the call, which is what an authored `@fallback`
/// responds to. With no fallback authored the work PARKS rather than quietly
/// running on a paid API (design D2, memql#4682).
///
/// There is no `auth` block, and that is the point of the whole epic: the
/// credential for a local model is owning the machine.
@base
@vendor("Fleet")
provider fleet { }

// ---- providers/v1/app/app.memql ----

/// A signed-in Claude Code or Codex on one of the user's own machines, as an
/// INFERENCE DOOR (epic memql#5096, design D3). One base provider, and
/// DELIBERATELY NO per-app children, for the reason the fleet has none: a
/// door exists while somebody is signed in on a machine that is awake, so a
/// registry entry written at load would be a claim this file cannot keep.
///
/// Apps are named from a policy as `app:<id>` (`@fallback("app:claude-code")`,
/// or `app:*` for any runnable one) and resolved against the machines whose
/// registration says the app is BOTH allowed and signed in.
///
/// THE INVERSION IS WHY IT SERVES WHAT IT SERVES. Everywhere else MemQL is
/// the agent and a model is its provider; here the app is the agent -- its own
/// loop, its own tools, its own judgement -- and MemQL is what it calls back
/// into over MCP. So this type serves chat and structured chat, where MemQL
/// hands over a prompt and takes back an answer, and NOT MemQL's own
/// tool-calling turns: driving an app that is itself driving produces two
/// agents fighting over one conversation.
///
/// Billing is `subscription`: the dollar ceiling excludes it because MemQL was
/// not billed, and the loop caps include it because a runaway loop on somebody
/// else's quota is still a runaway loop.
///
/// There is no `auth` block. The credential for an app door is a person having
/// signed into an app on a machine they own.
@base
@vendor("SubscriptionApp")
provider app { }

/// Speech input through the same federated credential as chat. Local audio
/// models remain first in the default policy and need no vendor connection.
@extends("openai")
@vendor("OpenAISTT")
@model("gpt-4o-mini-transcribe")
provider askTranscription {
  params { contextWindow 128000 }
}

/// Spoken replies. Voice selection is a per-session preference.
@extends("openai")
@vendor("OpenAITTS")
@model("gpt-4o-mini-tts")
provider askSpeech {
  params { contextWindow 128000 }
}
